      "shift": "22 Jan 2026, Shift 2",
      "status": "draft",
      "stem": "A capacitor $P$ with capacitance $10 \\times 10^{-6} \\mathrm{F}$ is fully charged with a potential difference of 6.0 V and disconnected from the battery. The charged capacitor $P$ is connected across another capacitor $Q$ with capacitance $20 \\times 10^{-6} \\mathrm{F}$. The charge on capacitor $Q$ when equilibrium is established will be $\\alpha \\times 10^{-5} \\mathrm{C}$ (assume capacitor $Q$ does not have any charge initially), the value of $\\alpha$ is $\\_\\_\\_\\_$。",
      "image": null,
      "options": [],
      "answer": {
        "min": 4.0,
        "max": 4.0
      },
      "solution": "Concept: after connection, charge redistributes until both capacitors have the same potential; total charge is conserved.\nInitial charge on $P$: $Q_0 = C_P V_0 = (10\\times10^{-6}\\ \\mathrm{F})(6.0\\ \\mathrm{V}) = 60\\times10^{-6}\\ \\mathrm{C}$\nCommon potential: $V = \\dfrac{Q_0}{C_P + C_Q} = \\dfrac{60\\times10^{-6}\\ \\mathrm{C}}{30\\times10^{-6}\\ \\mathrm{F}} = 2.0\\ \\mathrm{V}$\nCharge on $Q$: $q_Q = C_Q V = (20\\times10^{-6}\\ \\mathrm{F})(2.0\\ \\mathrm{V}) = 40\\times10^{-6}\\ \\mathrm{C} = 4\\times10^{-5}\\ \\mathrm{C}$\nCheck: charge left on $P$ is $20\\ \\mu\\mathrm{C}$, and $20+40=60\\ \\mu\\mathrm{C}$ ✓\nSo $\\alpha = 4$\nAnswer: 4"
    },
